NAME
       chrony.conf - settings of chronyd, the network time service

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/chrony.conf

DESCRIPTION
       chronyd keeps the system clock right by asking time servers over the
       network (NTP). It reads /etc/chrony.conf when it starts, so restart it
       after a change: systemctl restart chronyd. Each line is a directive
       and its values; # or ! starts a comment.

DIRECTIVES
       server NAME [iburst] [prefer]
              Use this time server. iburst sends a quick burst of requests at
              start, so the clock is set within seconds instead of minutes.
              prefer favours this server over others.

       pool NAME [iburst]
              Use a pool name that stands for many servers (RHEL 9 ships with
              "pool 2.rhel.pool.ntp.org iburst"). Comment it out when the
              machine has no internet access.

       driftfile FILE
              Where chronyd stores how fast or slow the clock runs.

       makestep THRESHOLD LIMIT
              Jump the clock at once if it is off by more than THRESHOLD
              seconds, during the first LIMIT updates; later it is only
              slowed down or sped up gently.

       rtcsync
              Copy the system time to the hardware clock regularly.

       allow NETWORK
              Act as a time server for these clients (for example
              allow 192.168.50.0/24). Also open the firewall: firewall-cmd
              --add-service=ntp.

       local stratum N
              Serve time even when no upstream server is reachable.

       logdir DIR
              Where to keep logs.

EXAMPLES
       Use the classroom time server:

           sed -i 's/^pool /#pool /' /etc/chrony.conf
           echo 'server ntp.lab.example.com iburst' >> /etc/chrony.conf
           systemctl enable chronyd
           systemctl restart chronyd
           chronyc sources -v

       In chronyc sources, the line starting with ^* is the server the clock
       is synchronised to now; ^? means the server cannot be reached yet.

FILES
       /etc/chrony.conf, /var/lib/chrony/drift

SEE ALSO
       chronyc(1), chronyd(8), timedatectl(1)
